Short:        Live system monitor for Kickstart 1.3
Author:       Paul Seibert (Desert Palms Software)
Uploader:     Paul Seibert <your@email.here>
Type:         util/moni
Version:      1.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 1.3
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     Nothing extra. Any Amiga, 512K is plenty.

SysMon 1.0 - (c) 2026 Desert Palms Software. Written by Paul Seibert.
Freeware.

SysMon is a small Workbench tool that shows you what your Amiga is doing,
live, in one window. It is written in C for AmigaOS 1.3 (V33) and runs
unchanged on 2.x and 3.x. It only uses the 1.3 operating system, so it
works on a stock A500 or A2000 as well as on bigger machines.

What it shows
-------------

 * Chip RAM and Fast RAM gauges: free and total memory, with the largest
   free block shown in the text report.
 * A rolling graph of memory use (chip in colour, fast as a dotted line).
 * Kickstart version, CPU and FPU, chipset (OCS / ECS / AGA) and PAL or
   NTSC, plus the number of running tasks.
 * Five views of the system lists, switched with the tabs or the View menu:
     Tasks      name, priority, state (Run / Ready / Wait), task or
                process, and for CLI processes the CLI number and the
                command that is running
     Libraries  name, version.revision and open count
     Devices    name, version.revision and open count
     Resources  name, plus version when the resource has one
     Ports      public message ports and the task each one signals
 * Click a row (or use the cursor keys) to select it. The status line
   shows its address and, for a task, the signals it is waiting for.

What it can do
--------------

 * Pri + / Pri -   change the selected task's priority, one step at a
                   time, within -20..+20.
 * Break           send the selected task a Ctrl-C (asks first). Most
                   CLI commands stop when they get one.
 * Project/Save report writes a full text report to RAM:SysMon.report.
 * Options menu: refresh every 1, 2 or 5 seconds; sort by name or by
   priority (remembered per view).

SysMon never kills or removes tasks. That is not safe on the Amiga, and a
system monitor should not be able to crash the system it is watching.
Before every action it checks, with multitasking stopped, that the task
it is about to touch is still there.

Using it
--------

From Workbench, double-click the SysMon icon. The window can be dragged,
sent to the back and made taller to show more rows. Right Amiga + Q quits.

From the CLI:

    sysmon            open the window
    sysmon REPORT     print the text report to the CLI and exit
    sysmon >RAM:sys.txt REPORT     the same, into a file

The REPORT mode is handy in scripts, or for sending someone your system
configuration when you ask for help.

Keyboard shortcuts (Right Amiga + key): A About, S Save report, Q Quit,
T L D R P switch view.

Installing
----------

Copy the SysMon drawer anywhere you like, for example to SYS:Utilities.
SysMon needs no libraries, fonts or assigns beyond the ROM.

Technical notes
---------------

 * Written in C, cross-compiled with GCC 6.5 (bebbo's amiga-gcc) against
   the Kickstart 1.3 includes, so it cannot call anything newer by
   accident. 22K, 68000 code.
 * System lists are copied out with Disable()/Forbid() held for as short
   a time as possible. Nothing is drawn or allocated while they are held.
 * Refreshes come from timer.device, so SysMon sleeps between updates and
   costs almost no CPU time.
 * The interface is built by hand from plain Intuition gadgets, with no
   GadTools, so it looks at home on 1.3 and uses the Workbench's own
   colours on 2.0 and later.
 * Tested on Kickstart 1.3 (A500, OCS, 512K + 512K) and Kickstart 3.1
   (ECS, 1MB + 1MB) under WinUAE, including a run-and-quit memory check:
   no memory is lost.

History
